  "query_preview": "(Today through Tuesday)...\nIssued at 224 AM EST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nWidespread rain is expected tonight through Thursday night, with \nfrom around 1 to near 2 inches of rain possible. Snow may mix in \nwith the rain Thursday night, but no accumulation is expected at \nthis time.\n\nEarly This Morning through Today...\n\nSome moisture is already being drawn north early this morning ahead \nof an upper level low, with a cloud deck around 6000FT already \napproaching the southern forecast area. This deck will continue to \nmove north through the early morning hours. Weak isentropic lift \nwill accompany this, with regional radar showing some weak echoes \nacross Kentucky.\n\nThe cloud deck will stop temperatures from falling, and readings may \nrise a bit south. The isentropic lift will weaken through the \nmorning, and any precipitation will have to overcome a dry lower \natmosphere. Will include some low PoPs in the far south from near \nsunrise through mid morning. With the warming effect of the clouds, \nexpect temperatures to be warm enough for precipitation type to be \nrain.\n\nDuring the afternoon, moisture will move northwest into the \napproaching upper system. Guidance has trended west with this plume \nof moisture, and better forcing will remain west of the area. Will \nhave some low PoPs in the far southwest and western forecast area \nthis afternoon.\n\nHighs today will be in the 40s, with coolest readings west where \nclouds will remain thicker longer.\n\nTonight through Friday...\n\nA deepening upper low will move from Missouri to Tennessee then \nnortheast through Friday. A cyclonically curved upper jet will \nremain south of the area. The associated surface low will remain \nsouth then east of central Indiana, but surface troughing will \nextend from the low back into the local area. Moisture looks to be \nplentiful.\n\nForcing from these will work with the moisture to provide widespread \nrainfall from overnight tonight through Thursday night. Thermal \nprofiles indicate only rain through the day on Thanksgiving. \nHowever, colder air will develop Thursday night into early Friday, \nwhich may allow some snow to mix in, especially in any heavier \nbanded precipitation. Wouldn't be surprised for isolated areas of \nminimal slushy snow accumulation, but at the moment am not confident \nto add any snow accumulation. Would not expect any impacts from the \nsnow at this time.\n\nReadings will warm enough by mid-morning Friday for lingering \nprecipitation to be rain, and rain chances will diminish from west \nto east during the day as forcing exits with the system.\n\nTotal rainfall amounts still look to be from near an inch up to 2 \ninches. Frontogenetical forcing may create pockets of heavier rain, \nbut given recent dryness, flooding is not a concern.\n\nHighs on Thanksgiving and Friday will be in the lower to middle 40s \nwith lows in the middle to upper 30s.\n\nGusty winds will accompany the system on Thanksgiving with gusts \nover 30 mph possible.\n\nSaturday and beyond...\n\nA couple of systems will move through nearly zonal flow aloft. Best \nforcing and moisture look to be on Sunday, so will go with likely \nPoPs then. Confidence is lower for a wave around Tuesday, so will \nkeep PoPs limited then. \n\nWith the nearly zonal flow, temperatures will be near normal to \nabove normal.",
